diff --git a/docs/dev/debt/shader.md b/docs/dev/debt/shader.md
index 2b2839ad3..649851945 100644
--- a/docs/dev/debt/shader.md
+++ b/docs/dev/debt/shader.md
@@ -52,7 +52,7 @@
 - Uint and Sint [image table](../../../core/shader/recompiler/SpirvBackend/src/SpirvMemory/SpirvDescriptors.cpp) entries and direct T#s under a table S# sample through a point-filtered copy of the S#: Vulkan leaves linear filtering of integer formats undefined; the console is assumed to point-sample.
 - Comparison sampling of a color texture through an [image table](../../../core/shader/recompiler/Optimization/src/ResourceMaterializer.cpp) S# throws at the first dispatch or draw that binds it.
 - An [image table](../../../core/shader/recompiler/SpirvBackend/src/SpirvImageEmitter.cpp) key with no key domain whose `key * stride` wraps past 2^32 to an offset between records faults only when the build already expects a fault; otherwise the fault buffer is not read and it reads zeros.
-- [Descriptor web paths](../../../core/shader/recompiler/Optimization/src/ResourceTracker.cpp) whose T# or S# dwords depend on the program counter, EXEC or a lane mask (registers left from `s_getpc_b64`, `s_mov_b64 exec` or a compare and its accumulation, typically on a loop's entry edge) select the first descriptor instead of throwing when the shader is compiled; the path is assumed not to reach the read, which is not proven, and the console would read through those values. A lane that takes such a path records an `InvalidDescriptor` BDA fault, but a shader that also uses an image table reads its fault buffer back only when a table record is poisoned, so the fault is lost there. Splits that substitute each incoming edge are not folded: a program-counter arm there stays a mandatory T#. PPSA23566 (UFC 6) needs this for compute shader 0x1163c84000. `APS5_STRICT_DESCRIPTOR_WEBS` makes such a read throw.
+- [Descriptor web paths](../../../core/shader/recompiler/Optimization/src/ResourceTracker.cpp) whose T# or S# dwords depend on the program counter, EXEC, a lane mask, the lane index or texels (registers left from `s_getpc_b64`, `s_mov_b64 exec`, a compare, `v_mbcnt` or an image read, often on a loop entry edge) select the first descriptor instead of throwing when the shader is compiled; the path is assumed not to reach the read, which is not proven, and the console would read through those values. A lane that takes such a path records an `InvalidDescriptor` BDA fault, but a shader that also uses an image table reads its fault buffer back only when a table record is poisoned, so the fault is lost there. Splits that substitute each incoming edge are not folded: a program-counter arm there stays a mandatory T#. PPSA23566 (UFC 6) needs this for compute shader 0x1163c84000, PPSA26344 (Ghost of Yōtei) for 0x800037fe00 (texels). `APS5_STRICT_DESCRIPTOR_WEBS` makes such a read throw.
 - [MaskedSelectEliminator](../../../core/shader/recompiler/Optimization/src/MaskedSelectEliminator.cpp) can discard inactive source lanes through `ReadFirstLane` only when its read mask implies the write mask and its result is unobserved outside the read mask. A loop-local read mask requires result uses in the same block without phi transport. The empty-EXEC lane-zero fallback follows AMD's [Vega ISA, section 12.8, V_READFIRSTLANE_B32, page 127](https://www.amd.com/content/dam/amd/en/documents/radeon-tech-docs/instruction-set-architectures/vega-shader-instruction-set-architecture.pdf) and the existing SPIR-V emitter. The optimization is covered by synthetic IR tests but has not been compared with console hardware; a proof that needs a read mask of a kind that is already being solved is refused.
 - [DenormalFlushEliminator](../../../core/shader/recompiler/Optimization/src/DenormalFlushEliminator.cpp) drops the result flush of a `v_mul_f32` or `v_mul_legacy_f32` product behind its nearest-even tininess check (see f32 denormals) when both factors are denormal-free: a product the check keeps is then zero, infinite, NaN or at least 2^-126 in magnitude, provided the driver rounds it as it rounds the check's multiply.
 - [Shader modules built at registration](../../../core/shader/recompiler/Recompiler.cpp) (shader recompiler): on devices with graphics pipeline libraries a vertex or pixel module reads buffer V# words, image modes, swizzles and mip ranges, depth-compare state, export mappings and vertex component selectors at run time instead of specializing on them per draw, so its libraries can be built when the shader is registered. A module that would exceed `SpirvTarget::registrationWordLimit` (200000 SPIR-V words) is emitted specialized as before: on RADV (Radeon 660M, Mesa 26.2.4) a sample of 60 pixel shaders of a retail title compiled 1.9 to 2.2 times slower in registration form below 141k words and 3.8 to 5.7 times slower above 280k words (none in between), so the limit sits in that gap; it was not measured on other drivers. Texel-offset folding, the format word of a buffer accessed with the descriptor's format (`buffer_load_format_*`; as runtime values every format's decoder is compiled, which made CI's lavapipe JIT ten times slower) and the vertex attribute numeric class stay specialized, so shaders with the first two, and pixel shaders whose image needs a multisampled descriptor, get no registration result; these, and every draw whose fixed-function state differs from the prebuild's, still build their libraries at the draw. Compute and the other stages stay specialized: in registration form the `agc_driver_dword_pattern_fill` compute shader hung CI's lavapipe (Mesa 25.2.8) when built with `ANYPS5_ENABLE_SPIRV_TOOLS=ON`, not on RADV, and the cause was not found.
